so i took a look at some questions from  a korean  university entrance exam and i was  pretty shocked by  how hard they were this is the csat  and i guess it's the south korean  version of the american sat  which you may be more familiar with but  i think this korean exam  ranks as much more difficult and is  right up there with some of the world's  toughest exams  it is taken by korean high school  students and the score determines  which university they can go to it is  very competitive  because many in korea believe that a  university's ranking  makes a huge difference to one's career  prospects  to get into those top unis you might  only be able to get one or two questions  wrong on the whole exam  but that becomes seemingly impossible  when you realize that the exam covers  five different topics on one day and  takes over eight hours to complete  i think one of the most difficult  sections is mathematics  it covers calculus with limits  derivatives  integration trig calculus even  integration by parts  plenty of conic sections vectors dot  products  3d geometry probability and statistics  and students would get three to four  minutes to solve  each question for question 15 we are  told  that two functions y equals a to the x  and y equals root three meet at the  point a  there's also this point b which is at  four zero  and we're asked what is the product of  all the values  of the little a such that the straight  lines  a b and o a  are perpendicular to each other now if  you ask me  that sounds pretty tricky i heard the  notorious questions hide near the end  so let's check out question 30 on this  2020 paper  what it says is that we have these two  functions one is a log  and one is an exponential and they meet  at  only one point when little a  can be said to be f of t it's a function  of this variable t  then we're asked to find the square of f  prime when t is equal to one-third  have a think about what you might want  to do here i'll come back at the end  and show my version of a solution but  first  let's look at some other sections  there's also  korean english history science  and some other optional papers the  english test starts off with a listening  section  and for this the whole country goes  silent  no planes take off or land in south  korea for that 30 minute period  so that shows how seriously this exam is  taken  for this recent question they had to  listen to what seems like a youtube  intro  and identify the purpose of the  paragraph  but later in the english section the  questions get a lot more abstract  with language that even a native english  speaker would find intense  there's a question about an african  proverb till the lions have their  historians  tales of hunting will always glorify the  hunter  and there's this paragraph here asking  us about the fact that  people don't usually think of touch as a  temporal phenomenon  but it is every bit as time-based as it  is spatial  the physics paper contains all the usual  suspects  but seems mostly based on conceptual  understanding  rather than heavy calculations  understanding  an eclipse how to determine the  wavelength of a laser  and orbital mechanics  here is just a glance at chemistry  biology and geology  and astrophysics  500 000 students take the exam each year  but around 20 of those are people taking  it again  because they weren't happy with their  previous score  those students may keep studying for the  same exam  year after year until they can get into  a good university  and move on with their life south korea  is an extremely well-educated country  their performance as well as enrollment  and graduation rates  are ranked among the top few in the  world they have around 80 percent of  high school graduates  going on to university so it may come as  no surprise that there is also  a booming private coaching industry  centered around passing this exam  they call them hagwons or cram schools  so let's take a look back at that math  problem it seems you first have to be  smart enough to figure out that if our  two functions would look like this  for the exponential and this for the log  that if they touch at only one point it  would be something like this  where the functions are tangent and do  not intersect  so we could set our function 1 equal to  function 2  to find that point where they touch that  will give us this  star equation here but that doesn't  immediately take us any further  instead we need a bit of a brain wave  that we can also  set the derivatives that is d y d  x equal to each other because at this  point  since they're tangent the slopes of each  line would be equal  so we can differentiate each side and  set them equal  noticing that our right hand side hasn't  changed during this process  we can replace it with the definition we  had for it earlier  giving us a version without any a and  just t's and  x's which can simplify down to give us  that  all of this is equal to one and i'll  call that  square because we're also going to use  that later  this might seem like another dead end  but we have to remember that we're being  asked to find  f prime of t which i prefer to think of  as d a d t and how would we get a d  a d t well we could go back up to  a equation that we started with and if  we were to differentiate  both sides of this with respect to t  this little a  in here due to implicit differentiation  would give us a  d a d t term so that's how we can get it  i differentiate both sides of it with  respect to t  remembering that a is a function of t  a bit of product rule a bit of chain  rule and you'll end up  with this now we're getting good at  replacing this term in here which crops  up again  i'm going to replace it with the result  of that initial differentiation  so i'll end up with this line here  now really we want to get d a dt by  itself since that's what we're  interested in  we can get rid of some of these terms by  multiplying both sides by  x minus t over t cubed and also  remembering that earlier we found  that all this was merely equal to one  because  that crops up right here we'll set that  equal to one  that simplifies things down quite a bit  a little bit more rearranging and we get  something for  d a d t which is the same as what  they're calling  f dash of t and because they wanted  f dash of t at t is equal to one third  we can put in one third for our value of  t  giving the minus eight and then they  want all of that squared  so that would be sixty-four perhaps the  calculus and algebra involved here  is not that advanced but the way that  it's arranged and the fact you need to  keep going back to results you found  earlier and somehow knowing how to get  your way out of these seemingly dead  ends  does make this problem really difficult  certainly a stretch to be able to do  this in just three or four minutes  and remember for this exam just thinking  that you can skip questions that are too  hard  is not really a feasible option if you  want to get those top scores  because getting just one or two wrong on  the whole thing  can actually make the difference if you  want to rank at the top  i think you're expected to be able to do  all of them best of luck to all  students studying for this exam or a  similar one  thanks for watching this video and you  can check out others in the playlist to  see how other countries might do things  thanks to my patreon supporters for  making this video possible  and a special shout out to today's  patron cat of the day  maeve
 

